ose to the 100 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (78). Bangor's all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on August 19, 1935.","t_chg":"Bangor's nights warmed over the long run. Its days have warmed since 1985.","p_chg":"Across 90 complete years: Bangor's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.21\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.06 to +0.35), about +2.1\u00b0F since 1926. Bangor's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.17 to +0.13) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1985, lows show no clear change and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.52\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.19 to +0.85). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; June fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Bangor's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.40 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 41.8 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (65.24 in); the driest, 2001 (24.58 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, 2 daily record lows"}}